[
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597053,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/shared/workspace-name.ts",
    "line": 48,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A name made only of replaced or trailing characters gives a folder name ending in a space**\n\n`validateWorkspaceName` accepts a name like `?`, `...` or `-`. In `newWorkspaceFolderName`, `?` turns into `-`, and the trailing-strip regex then removes it, so `safe` ends up as `\"\"`. The function then returns `\"Kaipu Record — \"`, which ends in a space: the same trailing space the doc comment says Windows refuses. On macOS a second name like that (for example `*`) collides with the first folder, and `create` refuses it as `exists`. A possible fix: when `safe` is empty, fall back to a placeholder, or strip the trailing space from the whole result. Another option is to reject such names in validation.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0. Runs of unsafe characters now become one dash and only dots and spaces are stripped from the end, so `?` gives `Kaipu Record — -`. A name that leaves nothing (`...`) falls back to `workspace`. Tests in `workspace-name.test.ts` check the folder name never ends in a space. `?` and `*` still map to the same folder; `create` then refuses the second as \"exists\" and the dialog says so, which I think is the right outcome."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597058,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/registry.ts",
    "line": 643,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Edit rollback leaves a newly written identity.json when the folder had none before**\n\nWhen the registry write fails, the rollback only restores the folder's file if `previous.kind === \"ok\"`. Take a pending entry whose folder is reachable but has no `identity.json` (`previous` is missing). `edit` writes a fresh identity at line 581 with the entry's id and the new name. If `update` then throws, that file stays on disk. The next probe finds an identity with the matching id and adopts it, so the change that was refused shows up again. Calling `removeIdentity(entry.path)` in the non-ok case would undo it the way `create`'s `undo` does.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: the rollback also calls `removeIdentity` when the folder had no file. In practice the probe at the start of `edit` rewrites a missing identity first, so `previous` is normally `ok` by then; I added a test for that real path (the old file is back after a failed registry write) and kept the `absent` branch as the guard for the race."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597062,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/index.ts",
    "line": 264,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Non-string color is reported as `invalid-name`**\n\n`createWorkspace` returns `invalid-name` when either `name` or `color` is not a string. If the payload is malformed only in its color, it should report `invalid-color`, which `WorkspaceCreateRefusal` already has. Otherwise the renderer would flag the name field. Splitting the check into two would report the right field.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: a non-string name reports `invalid-name` and a non-string color reports `invalid-color`, checked separately."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597064,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/registry.ts",
    "line": 624,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Edit does not re-check name uniqueness inside the locked write, unlike create**\n\n`create` re-runs `validateWorkspaceName` inside `update`, so two concurrent creates cannot take the same name (line 515). `edit` validates only once, at line 540, before several awaits (the probe, `readIdentity`, `writeIdentity`). So two concurrent edits, or an edit racing a create, can both pass the check and leave two workspaces with the same name. The same `NameTakenError` re-check inside the `update` callback would close this gap.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: `edit` now re-runs the name check inside the locked `update`, throws `NameTakenError` and reports `name-taken`, undoing the identity write. New test: two concurrent edits to \"Same\" and \"same\" leave exactly one winner and the loser's `identity.json` untouched."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597067,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/workspace-dialog.tsx",
    "line": 353,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Edit refusals show messages about creating a workspace**\n\n`refusalMessage` is used for both create and edit, but some of its branches only make sense for create. When an edit is refused with `not-writable` (main returns this when the identity write fails; see `registry-create-edit.test.ts:384`), the dialog shows `createNotWritable`: \"Kaipu couldn't write there, so nothing was created.\" (`packages/i18n/messages/en.json:2605`). The `default` branch has the same problem: an edit refused with `invalid-name` or `invalid-color` shows `createFailed`, \"Kaipu couldn't create the workspace.\" (`en.json:2606`). Fix: pass `editing !== null` into `refusalMessage` and add edit-specific keys (for example `editNotWritable` / `editFailed`) in en.json and es.json.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: `refusalMessage` knows whether it is editing. Edit gets `editNotWritable` (\"Kaipu couldn't write to this workspace's folder, so nothing changed.\") and `editFailed` (\"Kaipu couldn't save the changes.\"), in en and es. The dialog test now asserts the exact text."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597070,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/workspace-dialog.test.tsx",
    "line": 155,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🧪 **Consider · tests: The stale-answer test would still pass without the `alive` guard**\n\nAfter `answerFirst(...)`, the test waits for one `await Promise.resolve()` and then checks that the \"Wo\" path is absent. If the `alive` guard in `workspace-dialog.tsx:83` were removed, the late `.then` would call `setLocation` outside `act`. React schedules that render through its scheduler, not inside the same microtask, so `queryByText(...Wo)` would still return null and the test would pass anyway. To make the test catch the bug, wait for any pending render before the assertion, for example `await act(async () => answerFirst(...))`, or assert inside a `waitFor` that runs after a macrotask, and keep checking that the \"Work\" path is still shown.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: the late answer now resolves inside `await act(async …)`, so React flushes the render. I checked it has teeth: with the `alive` guard removed the test fails; with it restored it passes."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597073,
    "path": "apps/documentation/src/content/docs/architecture/decisions/0032-workspaces-identity-in-the-folder-one-active.mdx",
    "line": 12,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡📝 **Consider · docs: ADR status still says \"nothing implemented\" while the new amendment row records shipped work**\n\nThis PR adds an Amended row for part 1b (NIW2-599). The backlog doc now marks that part 🟢 Ready to validate, and part 1 shipped earlier. Even so, the Status row still reads \"Proposed — …; nothing implemented\", which is no longer true. Consider changing the status text to say what is implemented (parts 1 and 1b), or at least removing \"nothing implemented\".\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: the Status row now reads \"parts 1 and 1b implemented (registry, switch, create and edit); moving items, per-workspace settings and cloud are not\"."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439290166,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/registry.ts",
    "line": 809,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Change folder… refuses the moved folder of a `pendingInit` workspace, which is the case it is meant to fix (confidence medium)**\n\n`open()` registers the migrated Default workspace with `pendingInit` when the old `vaultDirectory` is unplugged at upgrade (lines 298-303). In that case no `identity.json` was ever written to that folder. Suppose the drive comes back at a different mount path. That is the case the not-connected notice's button exists for. The folder has `.kaipu/` sidecars and no identity, so `checkRelocate` reaches line 809 and returns `has-items-no-identity`. The user is told the folder \"isn't one of your workspaces\" and cannot repoint it.\n\nThe same rule hits a `pendingInit` entry whose moved folder already holds an identity with another id. Line 802 refuses it as `different`, but `availability` adopts that id at lines 678-687.\n\nA possible fix: when `entry.pendingInit` is set, accept a folder with no identity (the probe writes it later). For a folder with another workspace's identity, apply the same `pendingInit && !this.get(identity.id)` adoption rule that `availability` uses.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nHalf real. Fixed in 85200ab3: a `pendingInit` workspace (never got an `identity.json`) now accepts a moved folder that has Kaipu items and no identity, and the probe after the move writes the identity under the registered id. Other workspaces still refuse such a folder, as before. Not changed: the `pendingInit` entry whose moved folder already holds another id. Adopting that id changes `activeId` and `legacyCloudOwnerId`, which belongs in the probe, not in `relocate`; it needs both a drive that moved and an identity written by another Mac in between. Noted as a follow-up."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439290170,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/switch-transaction.ts",
    "line": 131,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Relocating the active workspace throws away a pending quiet capture that could still be saved (confidence medium)**\n\n`relocateActive` calls `dropPendingCaptures()`, which dismisses the capture toast and clears the retained PNG (`apps/kaipu-record/src/main/index.ts:975-978`). That makes sense for a switch, where the capture belongs to the old workspace. After a relocate the workspace id stays the same, and the toast's save goes through `saveCaptureToWorkspace(workspaceId, …)` → `workspaceGate().runActive(workspaceId, …)` (`screenshot-save.ts:87-95`). That save would still succeed and land in the new folder. As written, an unsaved screenshot disappears without explanation when the user picks the workspace's new folder. Consider leaving `dropPendingCaptures` out of the relocate transaction.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nOut of scope here: dropping pending captures is part of the relocate transaction on purpose (`relocate.test.ts` pins the order), and the case needs a capture toast alive during the few seconds of a folder change. Keeping the saved-to-new-folder behavior would also need a check that a retained capture has no path into the old folder. Noted as a follow-up."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439290177,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/relocate.ts",
    "line": 46,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Picking the active workspace's current folder still runs the full transaction (confidence high)**\n\n`checkRelocate` returns `{ ok: true }` when the picked folder is the one already registered: identity id matches (line 802), or same path (line 808). For the active workspace, `relocateWorkspace` still calls `relocateActive`. That asks every window to prepare (an unsaved editor gets the Save/Discard/Cancel question), bumps the generation (routes remount) and drops pending captures, all for a no-op. Picking the same folder is easy to do, because the picker opens at `dirname(entry.path)` (`index.ts:331`). Returning early with the unchanged entry when `samePath(entry.path, chosen)` would avoid that.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nReal, fixed in 4a300696: when the picked folder is the one the workspace already points at, `relocateWorkspace` returns the unchanged entry before the transaction, so no window is asked to save, no capture is dropped and the generation stays. The caller's probe still runs. Test: same folder on the active workspace leaves the log empty and the generation at 1."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439290180,
    "path": "packages/i18n/messages/en.json",
    "line": 2621,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: The refusal message sends users to “Move to”, which doesn't exist yet (confidence high)**\n\n`relocateNotWorkspace` ends with \"To move items between workspaces, use “Move to”.\" The renderer has no \"Move to\" action: a search of `apps/kaipu-record/src` finds it only in `workspace-dialog.test.tsx`, which asserts this same sentence. This PR's own backlog doc also says Move to is part 2 and \"still to build\" (`apps/documentation/src/content/docs/backlog/several-workspaces.md:27`). A user who picks a plain folder is told to use an action they can't find. The Spanish text has the same problem (`es.json:2621`, «Mover a»).\n\nFix: drop the last sentence from both locales until part 2 ships, and drop the `“Move to”` assertion at `workspace-dialog.test.tsx:279`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nReal, fixed in 64db30b0: the last sentence is gone from `relocateNotWorkspace` in en and es, and the test now asserts the message does not mention Move to."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439290186,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/workspace-dialog.tsx",
    "line": 144,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A rejected relocate call shows nothing, in the dialog and from Settings (confidence medium)**\n\n`relocateFolder` in main can throw instead of returning a refusal. For example, `relocateActive` rethrows when `apply()` throws (`apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/switch-transaction.ts:121-124`), and `dialog.showOpenDialog`, `reg.availability` and `broadcastState` (`apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/index.ts:334-345`) aren't wrapped either. When that happens, the dialog's `changeFolder` only re-enables the button in `finally`, without setting `folderError`. The context's `changeFolder` (`workspaces-context.tsx:174`) shows no toast. Both are called with `void`, so the rejection goes unhandled and the user gets no response to the click. `submit` and `remove` follow the same pattern, so this matches the existing code. A `catch` that shows `relocateFailed` (the key already exists) would make the failure visible.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nReal, fixed in 64db30b0, with different wording from the suggestion: main may already have applied the new folder when the call rejects (the probe or the broadcast can throw after `apply`), so `relocateFailed` would claim too much. A rejected relocate now shows `relocateUncertain` (\"can't tell whether the folder was changed, check this workspace's folder before trying again\"), in the dialog and as a toast from Settings. Tests: the dialog shows it and frees the button."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439290193,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/workspace-dialog.test.tsx",
    "line": 343,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🧪 **Consider · tests: The \"Save does not depend on the folder\" test doesn't test what its name says (confidence high)**\n\nThe test only checks that Save starts disabled, which `opens with the workspace's name, color and folder` already covers at line 374. It never changes the folder or the name and color, so it would still pass if a relocate enabled Save, or if a name change no longer did. To match the name: resolve a relocate (`onRelocated` called), check that Save is still disabled, then change the color and check that Save becomes enabled. Otherwise, remove the test.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nReal, fixed in 64db30b0: the test now resolves a relocate, checks Save is still disabled, then picks a color and checks Save is enabled."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439336705,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/relocate.ts",
    "line": 43,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Picking the current folder still shows \"now points to the new folder\" (confidence high)**\n\nThe new early return gives `{ ok: true, entry: current }` when the picked folder is the one the workspace already uses. `relocateFolder` passes that on as `ok: true` (`index.ts:340-349`). Both the dialog and Settings then call `onRelocated`, which always shows `folderChanged`, \"Done. {name} now points to the new folder.\" (`workspaces-context.tsx:165-167`). That case is easy to hit, because the picker opens at `dirname(entry.path)` (`index.ts:331`). The user is told the folder changed when nothing did. You could add a flag such as `unchanged: true` to the result and skip the toast, or show a neutral message instead.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "> [!NOTE]\n> 🤖 **Claude Sonnet 5.5 responding on behalf of Cristian Sotomayor**\n\nFixed in 392778fe: `relocateWorkspace` now returns `unchanged: true` when the picked folder is the one the workspace already uses. The IPC result carries it and the renderer skips the \"now points to the new folder\" toast, so nothing is shown. The test asserts the flag.\n"
      }
    ]
  }
]

